{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/a2-spain-part-1/audio/main-8bff9177faf0.mp3","durationSec":304.32,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":2.94,"text":"Most people in Spain live in flats, not houses."},{"start":3.64,"end":6.54,"text":"Their homes are often small, and the weather is often warm."},{"start":7.24,"end":8.9,"text":"So a lot of life happens outside."},{"start":9.7,"end":11.72,"text":"People meet friends in cafés and in the plaza."},{"start":12.34,"end":14.66,"text":"The plaza is the big square in the middle of a town."},{"start":15.08,"end":17.12,"text":"It is like a big living room for everyone."},{"start":17.66,"end":20.72,"text":"In summer, the afternoons are very hot, especially in the south."},{"start":21.32,"end":23.5,"text":"So people stay inside in the hottest hours."},{"start":24.02,"end":25.7,"text":"In the evening, the air gets cooler."},{"start":26.18,"end":28.84,"text":"So they go out for the paseo, a slow evening walk."},{"start":29.59,"end":33.28,"text":"Families walk through the town and say hello to neighbours."},{"start":33.28,"end":34.92,"text":"The paseo is not for sport."},{"start":35.3,"end":37.28,"text":"It is a way to see your town and your friends."},{"start":38.68,"end":41.92,"text":"Before 1492, Spain was not one country."},{"start":42.32,"end":43.38,"text":"It had several kingdoms."},{"start":44.04,"end":46.84,"text":"A kingdom is a country with a king or a queen."},{"start":47.66,"end":50.38,"text":"The biggest were Castile and Aragon."},{"start":50.82,"end":54.5,"text":"In the south was Granada, the last kingdom with Muslim rulers."},{"start":55.48,"end":60.1,"text":"Isabella was the future queen of Castile, and Ferdinand was the future king of Aragon."},{"start":60.9,"end":63.42,"text":"They married, so their kingdoms worked together."},{"start":64.48,"end":67.88,"text":"Isabella and Ferdinand are called the Catholic Monarchs."},{"start":68.46,"end":70.9,"text":"Monarch is another word for king or queen."},{"start":71.76,"end":74.1,"text":"In 1492, they took Granada."},{"start":75.12,"end":77.18,"text":"Now they ruled almost all of Spain."},{"start":77.84,"end":81.12,"text":"In the same year, they paid for the ships of a sailor called Columbus."},{"start":81.84,"end":86.64,"text":"He sailed west, and on 12 October 1492, he arrived in America."},{"start":86.64,"end":90.77,"text":"That is why 12 October is the national day of Spain."},{"start":90.76,"end":95.98,"text":"Spain has 17 regions, called autonomous communities."},{"start":97.22,"end":99,"text":"Each one has its own government."},{"start":99.78,"end":103.68,"text":"It decides many things for itself, like schools and hospitals."},{"start":104.56,"end":104.88,"text":"Why?"},{"start":105.56,"end":107.72,"text":"Long ago, Spain had several kingdoms."},{"start":108.32,"end":110.38,"text":"That is why the regions are still different today."},{"start":111.06,"end":112.68,"text":"Some even have their own language."},{"start":113.3,"end":115.3,"text":"Spanish is spoken all over Spain."},{"start":116.04,"end":119.68,"text":"People there often call it castellano, because it first came from Castile."},{"start":120.4,"end":123.92,"text":"Around Barcelona, in the north-east, people also speak Catalan."},{"start":124.72,"end":128.16,"text":"In the Basque Country, in the north, people also speak Basque."},{"start":129.04,"end":131.16,"text":"Most languages belong to a language family."},{"start":131.66,"end":134.46,"text":"It is a group of languages that grew from one old language."},{"start":135.14,"end":138.14,"text":"For example, Spanish and Catalan both grew from Latin."},{"start":138.94,"end":141.8,"text":"But Basque is not in the same family as any other language."},{"start":142.5,"end":145.88,"text":"So people who speak only Spanish cannot understand it."},{"start":146.62,"end":148.58,"text":"In Granada, you can see the Alhambra."},{"start":149.04,"end":151.02,"text":"It was the palace of the Muslim kings there."},{"start":151.8,"end":153.66,"text":"The name is Arabic for the red one."},{"start":154.24,"end":154.54,"text":"Why?"},{"start":155.18,"end":156.9,"text":"The walls are made of red earth."},{"start":157.66,"end":160.72,"text":"There is water everywhere, in long, quiet pools."},{"start":161.28,"end":165.76,"text":"In Barcelona, there is the Sagrada Família, a very big church by Gaudí."},{"start":165.76,"end":169.86,"text":"Building started in 1882, and it is still not finished."},{"start":170.36,"end":171.7,"text":"Why is it so slow?"},{"start":171.7,"end":174.18,"text":"The church gets no money from the government."},{"start":174.18,"end":177.06,"text":"Tickets and gifts from visitors pay for the work."},{"start":177.56,"end":179.34,"text":"So it can only grow when there is money."},{"start":180.2,"end":182.34,"text":"Today it is the tallest church in the world."},{"start":183.64,"end":185.22,"text":"Lunch is the biggest meal of the day."},{"start":185.94,"end":187.88,"text":"People eat it at about 2 pm."},{"start":187.88,"end":190.22,"text":"Dinner comes late, at 9 pm or later."},{"start":190.82,"end":191.04,"text":"Why?"},{"start":192.4,"end":195.7,"text":"Until 1940, the clocks in Spain were right for the sun."},{"start":196.32,"end":200.3,"text":"In 1940, the dictator Franco moved the clocks one hour forward."},{"start":201,"end":203.16,"text":"He wanted Spain to have the same time as Germany."},{"start":203.98,"end":205.88,"text":"A dictator is a ruler with all the power."},{"start":206.44,"end":208.18,"text":"But people did not change their day."},{"start":208.68,"end":209.98,"text":"They still live by the sun."},{"start":211.52,"end":214.1,"text":"So on the clock, lunch and dinner look late."},{"start":214.96,"end":215.92,"text":"Lunch is also long."},{"start":216.42,"end":216.68,"text":"Why?"},{"start":217.12,"end":219.82,"text":"It is the biggest meal, so people eat it slowly."},{"start":220.66,"end":224.16,"text":"Many small shops close from about 2 pm to 5 pm."},{"start":224.16,"end":226.78,"text":"So the owners can go home and eat with their family."},{"start":227.62,"end":230.84,"text":"After the meal, people stay at the table and talk."},{"start":231.88,"end":236.3,"text":"There is a word for this time: sobremesa, or over the table."},{"start":237.44,"end":240.46,"text":"A famous lunch dish is paella, from Valencia."},{"start":241.32,"end":244.1,"text":"It is rice cooked in a wide, flat pan."},{"start":245.02,"end":249.04,"text":"In Valencia, people call this pan a paella."},{"start":249.4,"end":251.74,"text":"So the dish has the same name as the pan."},{"start":252.7,"end":255.3,"text":"Paella began as lunch for farmers in the rice fields."},{"start":255.86,"end":258.08,"text":"Every spring, Seville has its big fair."},{"start":258.48,"end":259.38,"text":"It lasts about a week."},{"start":260.14,"end":263.44,"text":"It started as a market for buying and selling farm animals."},{"start":264.4,"end":268.3,"text":"Today it is like a small town of colourful tents along long streets."},{"start":269.78,"end":271.66,"text":"Each tent is called a caseta."},{"start":272.52,"end":276.56,"text":"Most of them belong to families, groups of friends, clubs or companies."},{"start":277.38,"end":280.22,"text":"So you usually go in only when the owners invite you."},{"start":281.06,"end":286.12,"text":"Inside, people eat, drink and dance sevillanas, a happy dance for couples."},{"start":287.14,"end":289.7,"text":"Seville is in Andalusia, the home of flamenco."},{"start":290.56,"end":294,"text":"Flamenco is singing, guitar music and dance together."},{"start":294.9,"end":297.58,"text":"The dancer often hits the floor hard with the shoes."},{"start":298.06,"end":299.72,"text":"So the shoes make music too."},{"start":300.76,"end":303.9,"text":"The Romani people of Andalusia helped to create flamenco."}],"words":[{"text":"Most","start":0,"end":0.3},{"text":"people","start":0.3,"end":0.62},{"text":"in","start":0.62,"end":0.86},